   "title": "Quadratic Inequalities & Regions",
   "content": "<p><strong>Quadratic inequalities</strong> like \\(x^2 - 5x + 6 < 0\\) are solved by factorising, finding the critical values (roots), then sketching the parabola to determine where it's positive or negative.</p><p>For \\(x^2\\) coefficient positive: the parabola is U-shaped. It's <strong>below zero</strong> (negative) <em>between</em> the roots, and <strong>above zero</strong> (positive) <em>outside</em> the roots.</p><p><strong>Set notation:</strong> \\(x^2 - 5x + 6 < 0\\) → \\((x-2)(x-3) < 0\\) → \\(2 < x < 3\\). But \\(x^2 - 5x + 6 > 0\\) → \\(x < 2\\) or \\(x > 3\\).</p><p><strong>Regions on a graph</strong> are areas satisfying multiple inequalities. Shade the wanted region (or unwanted — the question will specify). Lines are solid for \\(\\leq / \\geq\\) and dashed for \\(< / >\\).</p>",
   "example": "<p><strong>Solve</strong> \\(x^2 - x - 12 > 0\\)</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Factorise: \\((x-4)(x+3) > 0\\)</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Roots: \\(x = 4\\) and \\(x = -3\\)</p><p><strong>Step 3:</strong> U-shape, > 0 means outside: \\(x < -3\\) or \\(x > 4\\)</p>"
  },
